# Future Improvements

Folio currently demonstrates how daily human-curated music Editions could help people discover and discuss a local music scene. Future development would focus on making participation easier, strengthening trust and safety, and testing whether the experience supports meaningful connections over time.

## Improve Onboarding and Navigation

Our evaluation showed that both participants struggled to find how to comment. We added a Discussion shortcut and clearer guest participation prompts, but these changes need further testing.

We would improve onboarding with a short explanation of daily Editions, user roles and participation options. Clearer navigation should also help users distinguish music discovery, nearby events and discussion. New users should understand what Folio offers without needing instructions from the development team.

## Connect Venues, Events and Music Picks

One participant interpreted Browse as an events guide, highlighting an opportunity to connect event discovery more clearly with the daily Edition. Linking venues and gigs back to featured artists would help users move from reading about music to exploring where they could experience it locally.

This connection is not currently built. Future development could introduce links between picks, artist information and relevant events. Event details would also need a reliable process for checking dates, locations and cancellations.

## Build Moderation and Reporting Tools

Comment moderation is an important priority before wider public use. We would introduce reporting tools, community guidelines and a moderation interface so harmful content can be reviewed consistently.

Users should receive clear explanations when their content is removed and have a way to challenge a decision. We would also test safeguards against spam and repeated abuse while preserving the accessibility of guest participation.

## Strengthen Curator Accountability

Curators shape the identity and credibility of Folio. We would make application criteria clearer and develop curator profiles that explain their musical interests and connections to the local scene.

We would also introduce conflict-of-interest disclosures and review representation across Editions. These improvements would help users understand whose perspective they are reading and help us identify artists, genres or communities that receive limited attention.

## Improve Source Checking and Corrections

Although Folio requires sources before publication, future development should support better verification. We would strengthen editorial guidance, cross-check seed content and make supporting sources easier for users to inspect.

A correction process would allow artists and listeners to flag inaccurate information. Published Editions should retain a visible record of significant amendments so that the midnight lock does not prevent responsible corrections.

## Develop Privacy and Account Management

The prototype allows reading without an account and guest participation without an email address, but account management remains limited. We would introduce clearer privacy information, account deletion and options for managing or removing contributions.

For users who choose an email-based account, a secure recovery process would improve reliability. We would also review data retention and location handling so that information is collected and stored only where necessary.

## Expand Accessibility

We would carry out a broader accessibility review covering screen-reader support, keyboard navigation, colour contrast, text resizing and touch interactions. The clearer discussion prompts and larger tap targets already added would be tested with users to assess whether they resolve the original problem.

Further evaluation should include people with different abilities, language backgrounds and levels of digital confidence. We would also simplify unfamiliar terminology and provide enough context for people who are new to Brisbane’s music scene.

## Refine Social Features

Near-live comments, sharing and the anonymous “reading now” count were added after participant evaluation. Future testing should examine whether these features help users feel connected and whether they introduce confusion or pressure.

The reading count needs protection against inflation through repeated calls before it can provide dependable information. We would also assess whether the current five-second polling approach provides a useful experience without unnecessary network activity.

## Integrate an Interactive Map

We would add an interactive map to help users explore music venues and upcoming gigs across Brisbane. Map markers could link to venue details, relevant events and featured artists, connecting Folio’s daily Editions with opportunities to experience local music in person.

Users could filter locations by event date, music genre or distance. Selecting a marker would show the venue’s address, available accessibility information and links to event details or directions. A list view would provide an alternative for users who find maps difficult to navigate.

Location access would remain optional, allowing users to search by suburb or move around the map manually. We would also need to check venue and event information regularly and clearly identify missing or unverified details.

## Support Artist Participation

We would explore ways for artists to suggest factual corrections, submit relevant information and manage how they connect with listeners. These options should support participation while keeping editorial selection independent.

Any artist submission process would need clear review criteria and should not imply guaranteed inclusion in an Edition. Artists should also have control over their contact boundaries and a straightforward way to raise concerns about their representation.

## Expand Beyond Brisbane Carefully

Folio currently focuses on Brisbane, with other cities marked as “Coming soon.” Expansion would require more than adding city names or copying existing content. Each city would need credible local curators, relevant sources and an understanding of its communities.

We would first test whether the experience remains useful over time in Brisbane. Any expansion should then be gradual, with local input and sufficient moderation and editorial capacity to support each additional city.

## Improve Technical Reliability and Security

Before broader release, we would extend security, performance and scale testing. This would include checking role permissions, account access, concurrent commenting and daily Edition transitions across city time zones.

Our existing automated tests provide a foundation, but simultaneous multi-user use was not tested during participant evaluation. We would therefore test the app with several users interacting at once and assess how it handles interrupted connections, failed requests and increased activity.

## Conduct Broader and Longer-Term Evaluation

Our two short evaluation sessions provided useful formative feedback, but they cannot establish whether Folio supports lasting engagement or community connection. Future research should involve a larger and more varied group of listeners, newcomers, artists and curators.

Longer-term trials would help us understand whether users return for daily Editions, discover unfamiliar artists and participate in useful discussions. We would also evaluate features added after the initial sessions and use the findings to decide which features to improve, simplify or remove.
